Each row after the first row is formed by placing a 1 at each end of the row, and each interior entry is 1 greater than the sum of the two numbers diagonally above it in the previous row. What is the units digits of the sum of the 2023 numbers in the 2023rd row?

<math>\textbf{(A) } 1 \qquad \textbf{(B) } 3 \qquad \textbf{(C) } 5 \qquad \textbf{(D) } 7 \qquad \textbf{(E) } 9</math>

FORUM SOLUTION:
I guess that here’s one of the a bit more rigorous solution

Define $dp(i)$ as the sum of the ith row, we observe that $dp(i)=2dp(i-1) + n - 2$. This is true because every term in the ith row is used twice when calculating the i+1th row (except for the two 1s). The trick is to add $n$ to both sides to get $dp(i)+n=2dp(i-1) + 2n - 2$. We define a new sequence $a_n = dp(n) + n$. Plug it into the original recursion we get $a_n = 2a_{n-1}$. We calculate that $a_1 = 2$ which means that $a_{2023} = 2^{2023}$. Thus $dp(i) = 2^{2023 }- 2023$

PROBLEM: In triangle <imath>ABC</imath>, <imath>AB = 10</imath>, <imath>BC = 14</imath>, and <imath>CA = 16</imath>. Let <imath>D</imath> be a point on segment <imath>\overline{BC}</imath>. Let points <imath>I_B</imath> and <imath>I_C</imath> denote the incenters of triangles <imath>ABD</imath> and <imath>ACD</imath>, respectively. The circumcircles of triangles <imath>BI_BD</imath> and <imath>CI_CD</imath> meet at distinct points <imath>P</imath> and <imath>D</imath>. The maximum possible area of <imath>\triangle BPC</imath> can be expressed in the form <imath>a - b\sqrt {c}</imath>, where <imath>a</imath>, <imath>b</imath>, and <imath>c</imath> are positive integers and <imath>c</imath> is not divisible by the square of any prime. Find <imath>a + b + c</imath>.

FORUM SOLUTION:
Using Law of Cosines, we find that $\angle BAC = 60^\circ$. Also, notice

\begin{align*}
\angle BPC &= \angle BPD +\angle CPD \\
&= (180^\circ - \angle BI_BD) + (180^\circ- \angle CI_CD) \\
& = 360^\circ - \angle BI_BD - \angle CI_CD \\
& = 360^\circ - \left (90^\circ + \frac{1}{2} \angle BAD \right) - \left (90^\circ + \frac{1}{2} \angle CAD \right) \\
& = 180-\frac{1}{2} \angle BAC = 150^\circ.
\end{align*}

Since $\angle BPC$ is fixed, the optimal point for $P$ is when $BP=PC=k$. Thus, by Law of Cosines, we have

\[14^2=k^2+k^2-2 \cdot k \cdot k \cos(150^\circ)\]
\[\implies k^2 = 196(2-\sqrt{3}).\]

The area is thus,

\[\frac{1}{2}k^2 \sin(150^\circ) = 98-49\sqrt{3} \implies \boxed{150}.\]

